588-3.3 The ‘s’ Command
589-===================
590-
591-The ‘s’ command (as in substitute) is probably the most important in
592-‘sed’ and has a lot of different options.  The syntax of the ‘s’ command
593-is ‘s/REGEXP/REPLACEMENT/FLAGS’.
594-
595-   Its basic concept is simple: the ‘s’ command attempts to match the
596-pattern space against the supplied regular expression REGEXP; if the
597-match is successful, then that portion of the pattern space which was
598-matched is replaced with REPLACEMENT.
599-
600-   For details about REGEXP syntax *note Regular Expression Addresses:
601-Regexp Addresses.
--
833-
834-     Instances of the ‘/’ (or whatever other character is used in its
835-     stead), ‘\’, or newlines can appear in the SOURCE-CHARS or
836-     DEST-CHARS lists, provide that each instance is escaped by a ‘\’.
837-     The SOURCE-CHARS and DEST-CHARS lists _must_ contain the same
838:     number of characters (after de-escaping).
839-
840-     See the ‘tr’ command from GNU coreutils for similar functionality.
841-
842-‘a TEXT’
843-     Appending TEXT after a line.  This is a GNU extension to the
844-     standard ‘a’ command - see below for details.
845-
846-     Example: Add ‘hello’ after the second line:
847-          $ seq 3 | sed '2a hello'
848-          1
849-          2
850-          hello
851-          3
852-
853-     Leading whitespace after the ‘a’ command is ignored.  The text to
854-     add is read until the end of the line.
855-
856-‘a\’
