1966-=============================================
1967-
1968-A “bracket expression” is a list of characters enclosed by ‘[’ and ‘]’.
1969-It matches any single character in that list; if the first character of
1970:the list is the caret ‘^’, then it matches any character *not* in the
1971-list.  For example, the following command replaces the strings ‘gray’ or
1972-‘grey’ with ‘blue’:
1973-
1974-     sed  's/gr[ae]y/blue/'
1975-
1976-   Bracket expressions can be used in both *note basic: BRE syntax. and
1977-*note extended: ERE syntax. regular expressions (that is, with or
1978-without the ‘-E’/‘-r’ options).
1979-
1980-   Within a bracket expression, a “range expression” consists of two
1981-characters separated by a hyphen.  It matches any single character that
1982-sorts between the two characters, inclusive.  In the default C locale,
1983-the sorting sequence is the native character order; for example, ‘[a-d]’
1984-is equivalent to ‘[abcd]’.
